        {
          "type": "section",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "level": 2,
              "text": "Two medians, and they are not the same statistic"
            },
            {
              "type": "richtext",
              "html": "\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E62.5 across 1 language with a field guide\u003C/strong\u003E is the middle of the per-language medians: every language counts once, whether its field holds four hundred codebases or five.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E60.5 across 10 published measured codebases\u003C/strong\u003E is the middle of the codebases themselves, so the largest fields pull it hardest.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003EBoth are true of the same corpus and they will not agree. A median quoted without the population it was taken over is one a reader cannot check, so each of these carries its own: 10 published measured codebases, measured 22 September 2026 at 00:00 UTC.\u003C/p\u003E"
            }
          ],
          "appearance": "Note",
          "anchor": "medians"
